00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.719
Supersonic speed, modular cabin design, all
electric power, transparent cabin walls, pilotless

00:00:14.719 --> 00:00:19.170
planes, personal jets for everyone—that
all sounds great, but what’s actually the

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.300
plane of the future.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.869
In the next 15-20 years, what will be the
next major aircraft release to make an impact

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:26.869
on the industry?

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:32.250
Well, it’s not nearly as exciting as those
concepts, at least for passengers.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:37.040
More and more people travel by plane each
year and the industry is only growing.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.260
In recent years the Boeing 787 Dreamliner
has made a significant impact on the airline

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.260
industry.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.649
This relatively small, super-efficient, long
range airplane has allowed for the advent

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:52.860
of long-haul budget airlines and for traditional
airlines to open up long-haul routes between

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.980
smaller markets.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.660
Now, the near-future of aviation has a lot
to do with one decision Boeing made almost

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.210
a decade ago—a decision they’ve been regretting
ever since.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:09.610
In 1983 the first Boeing 757 entered into
service with Eastern Airlines.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:14.560
It was billed as a replacement to the successful
727 for popular shorter routes that needed

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.920
more seats than a 737.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:23.280
At the time it was incredibly fuel efficient
burning 40% less fuel per seat than its predecessor.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.910
It sold well, but not phenomenally.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:30.610
By the turn of the millennium about a thousand
were made and sales were slowing rapidly so

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.170
Boeing pulled the plug on the aircraft’s
production in 2004.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:39.410
But then, in 2007, the aircraft started to
be used for something it wasn’t intended

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.860
for—transatlantic service.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:47.180
When the 757 was originally developed airlines
weren’t really allowed to fly twin-engine

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:52.530
aircraft over long stretches of ocean, but
the regulations changed so airlines changed

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.080
how they used the plane.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.490
With a range of well over 4,000 miles, the
757 can easily reach most destinations in

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.330
western Europe from the US east coast.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:08.110
When operating a smaller plane, airlines are
almost guaranteed that they can fill the seats

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.670
so they’re almost guaranteed to make money.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.140
So, instead of operating larger planes that
might not fill up, airlines started flying

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.000
this single-aisle, twin-engine plane across
the Atlantic.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.300
The smaller size also allowed airlines to
fly to smaller destinations with smaller demand.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.820
United, for example, flies this plane on routes
like New York to Shannon, Birmingham, Edinburgh,

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.620
Lisbon, and Stockholm.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.920
At the same time, since the plane was originally
developed for domestic routes, it can easily

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.150
be used for shorter domestic flights between
transatlantic flights which leads to high

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.540
aircraft utilization—a key to profitability.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.760
But these planes are getting old.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.210
Some have been in service for well over 30
years and airlines need to retire them to

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:57.710
remain competitive both with passenger comfort
and with aircraft efficiency and right now,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.290
nobody really knows what’s going to replace
these aging 757’s.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.319
There’s a huge gap in the market.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:09.710
The biggest Boeing single aisle aircraft in
production is the 737 Max 10 while the smallest

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.040
twin-aisle aircraft is the 787-8 Dreamliner.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:19.480
In an all economy configuration the Max 10
carries 230 passengers while the Dreamliner

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.300
carries 359 passengers.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:28.180
Meanwhile, the Max 10 can fly up to 3,700
miles while the Dreamliner can fly up to 8,300

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:29.180
miles.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:36.950
At between 230 and 280 passengers and up to
4,000 nautical miles, the 757 perfectly fits

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:42.200
between the two sizes, but we need a plane
of the future for this middle spot.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:47.110
Neither Boeing or Airbus has officially announced
plans for a middle of the market plane but

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.880
it's almost certain that the next entirely
new plane to hit the market will be in this

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.590
sweet spot of size.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:59.420
Boeing tried to hurriedly make a plane this
size with the 737-MAX program but many traditional

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.500
airlines aren’t buying it.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.940
Budget airlines love using it to serve between
the British Isles and US east coast, but the

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:13.200
largest 737-MAX can only fly up to 3,700 miles—only
barely reaching continental Europe from New

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.200
York.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:20.010
United, Delta, and American Airlines all want
a longer-range and slightly larger plane for

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.820
their low-demand transatlantic routes.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.190
So here’s the good news: this plane is coming.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.870
There have only been trickles of information,
but we’ve all but confirmed that the Boeing

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:38.160
797 will be a brand new plane between the
size of the 737 and the 787 and here’s what

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.160
it will look like.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:46.720
It will have between 225 and 260 seats and
a range of just shy of 6,000 miles—enough

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.500
to get even to the American west coast from
London.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.620
While the increased range of the 737-Max planes
is allowing transatlantic service from smaller

00:04:54.620 --> 00:05:00.300
cities in the British Isles and American east
coast, this 797 will open transatlantic service

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:06.050
from cities deeper into the continents along
with replacing the aging 757s.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:10.550
With super-efficient engines and composite
design, it will be one of the most efficient

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:14.760
planes yet and further drive down the cost
of hopping the pond.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.870
Meanwhile, Airbus is doing nothing, because
they already tried.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:26.150
The Airbus a310 was exactly the size of this
potential 797 but they only sold 250 of them.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:30.710
It was likely before its time—airlines just
didn’t consider serving long-haul routes

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.930
between smaller markets at the time.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.880
It’s a newer concept.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:41.680
Ironically, the best replacement for the Boeing
757 is actually an Airbus plane—the a321

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.940
neo long range—but this plane is a bit too
small to serve as a true replacement.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:51.400
It’s more a temporary solution until Boeing
releases their solution.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:57.370
As of now the 797 would most likely enter
service in 2025 so it will be a while until

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.300
we know which manufacturer chose correctly.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:01.900
But what’s next?

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.790
After completing their entire ranges of aircraft
sizes, what will Boeing and Airbus make to

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.400
remain competitive?

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.590
Right now oil prices are low so there’s
less pressure to operate greener aircraft,

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:18.900
but most analysts predict a doubling of oil
prices in the next 20-30 years with ever faster

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.740
increases after that.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.979
The industry will need to adapt to stay alive
against the potential future threat of high-speed

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:28.940
land-based transportation systems like hyperloop.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.750
For that, electric is the answer.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:36.690
Fuel is airlines’ number one cost and cutting
that out could make flying competitive in

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:42.070
price to taking the bus, but electric aircraft
have their limitations.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:46.449
Current batteries are heavy and electric engines
are weak so electric aircraft are limited

00:06:46.449 --> 00:06:51.910
in speed and range, but there’s a place
for that kind of aircraft—on regional routes.

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.919
Trains currently dominate the 200-400 mile
transportation market.

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:59.560
Think DC to New York, Edinburgh to London,
or Tokyo to Osaka.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.950
There’s just no way that the plane can beat
the train in terms of price, convenience,

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:08.800
and time, but the electric aircraft has the
potential to beat the train on the most important

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.430
of those three factors—price.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:16.350
By offering a transportation option at roughly
the same cost but significantly faster than

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:21.669
the bus, electric aircraft will have the potential
to dominate the short-haul market because

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:25.540
on shorter routes, aircraft speed doesn’t
really matter.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:29.380
On flights so short, aircraft barely get up
to their top speeds.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:34.680
Both British Airways and Flybe operate the
Edinburgh to London Heathrow route, for example,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:42.270
but British Airways uses a320 and 767 jets
while Flybe uses Bombardier Dash 8 turboprops.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:47.479
British Airways flies this route in an average
79 minutes while Flybe, with their slower

00:07:47.479 --> 00:07:53.440
turboprop planes flies between Edinburgh and
London Heathrow in a faster 73 minutes on

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.440
average.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:08:01.590
British Airways’ a320 has a top speed of
541 mph while Flybe’s dash 8 has a top speed

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:06.820
of 370 miles per hour and yet the turboprop
flies the route faster.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:11.120
On such a short route, the low speed of an
electric airplane wouldn’t matter since

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.600
aircraft can’t get up to speed and the dramatic
cost reduction would make airplanes the absolute

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:20.139
dominant transportation method in short-haul
markets.

00:08:20.139 --> 00:08:24.800
Airbus is the current market leader in electric
aircraft development with a goal to release

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.380
a hybrid commuter aircraft by 2030, but as
the reality of a world without oil nears,

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:35.459
Boeing will likely start more serious development
of an electric aircraft in the coming years.

00:08:35.459 --> 00:08:39.429
None of this is to say that we will never
see some of the more exciting future aircraft

00:08:39.429 --> 00:08:44.470
concepts, but the entire aviation industry
is dictated by economics, not comfort.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:50.209
It’s a lot easier for airlines to market
lower prices than it is for them to sell higher

00:08:50.209 --> 00:08:54.160
comfort so more efficient aircraft will always
win.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:58.300
With the completion of a middle-of-the-market
airplane, however, the entire range of aircraft

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:03.420
sizes will be filled in a way that might allow
the aircraft manufacturers to turn their focus

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:08.499
to developing more novel aircraft concepts
that will change the way we fly in the future.

00:09:08.499 --> 00:09:12.709
The good news is that these aircraft developments
are all about making flying cheaper which

00:09:12.709 --> 00:09:16.450
will let more and more people fly for less.

